WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.980
Token security depends on key management.

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:10.360
JWT signing keys, validation keys, and Data Protection keys are not background details.

3
00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:17.260
They determine whether tokens can be issued, validated, protected, or invalidated as intended.

4
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:22.400
A well-designed token format can still fail operationally if keys are poorly managed.

5
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:29.240
JWT signing keys and validation keys must be protected, rotated, and distributed carefully.

6
00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.660
Services that issue tokens need access to signing material.

7
00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:39.920
APIs that validate tokens need the right validation keys and metadata.

8
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:46.740
Rotation should be planned so active tokens remain valid only as intended and stale keys are retired safely.

9
00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:52.140
Data Protection key rings need persistence and protection appropriate to the hosting environment.

10
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:56.880
A single-server development setup behaves differently from a scaled-out production deployment.

11
00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:08.020
Containers, ephemeral storage, deployment slots, shared file systems, cloud key storage, and managed services can all affect key persistence and access.

12
00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:12.940
Development keys, staging keys, and production keys should be separated.

13
00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:19.760
Environment-specific keys reduce blast radius and prevent test behavior from affecting production token validation.

14
00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:25.860
The deployment pipeline should make it hard to mix key rings or signing material across environments by accident.

15
00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:33.820
Key compromise response should include containment, rotation, revocation decisions, monitoring, and communication.

16
00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:43.520
The response may need to account for active tokens, refresh tokens, user sessions, service clients, integration partners, and operational dependencies.

17
00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.740
The plan should exist before a real incident.

18
00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:56.700
Secrets and keys should not be stored in source control, public configuration, build logs, downloadable artifacts, or broad administrative interfaces.

19
00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:04.960
Key access should be scoped, audited, and owned by the teams responsible for identity, platform, and application security.
